osx-gpu: radeonsi on a big-endian host, part two: host-order buffers, GPU-order textures

Application shaders swap every 16-, 32- and 64-bit element after a UBO,
SSBO, global or constant load and before a store (si_nir_lower_be_memory,
run before radeonsi's own memory loads, the vertex-buffer descriptors of
the vertex-input lowering and the T#s and S#s of the resource lowering,
which use the same intrinsics and must stay little-endian, and before the
access-size lowering so each access is seen at its own size); user
constant buffers are no longer converted to little-endian on upload, so
both kinds of constant buffer agree; radeonsi's own shaders, marked by name
in si_create_shader_state, are left alone, they work on what the hardware
or the driver wrote. A texture of a format wider than bytes keeps the
GPU's layout and is mapped only through a staging texture, swapped unit by
unit after the copy in and before the copy out (si_be_swap_unit), so the
CPU sees Mesa's host-order layout and the GPU its own: Core Image's RGBA16
and float pbuffers, which the window server composites with, work without
any CPU access at all. Measured on the W8100, 2026-11-04: the
fixed-function matrices arrived byte-reversed and no vertex reached the
screen; a build that swapped the descriptor loads drew black vertex
colours and hung the sampler; and with 16-bit formats refused to the
sampler the window server's pbuffer calls failed (BadDrawable, BadState)
and Core Image fell back to software.

Amended with part six (2026-11-13): si_be_swap_unit swaps the two packed
float formats (R11G11B10, R9G9B9E5), which are not "plain" but are a
host-order word in Mesa's view all the same, and the 24-bit depth channel
of Z24X8 and X8Z24 by its whole word (it took a unit of 3, which swapped
nothing); 8-bit blocks need no staging. si_be_withhold_packed() reads
RDN_BE_PACKED for the gates in si_state.c.

